# Operating principles — read these two files

Cursor has no global instruction file, and it only walks ancestors for
`AGENTS.md`, so a project outside `$HOME` sees none of the shared context that
every other CLI on this machine loads automatically. This rule closes that gap.

**At the start of any non-trivial task, read both of these:**

1. `~/dev/weft-fabric/principles/AGENTS.md` — the shared operating
   principles. Identical on every machine and every CLI (Claude Code, Codex,
   Gemini, Cursor). It covers how to iterate, when to interrupt the user, how
   secrets are handled, and the shared skill library.

2. `~/.config/agents/MACHINE.md` — what is true on *this* machine only: which
   tools are actually installed, which hosts are reachable and how, and which
   of the shared rules cannot run here. **It overrides anything in the shared
   file that does not apply locally.**

They are a pair. The first is byte-identical everywhere by design; the second is
never synced. If a shared rule names a path or command this box does not have,
the machine file says what to do instead — do not edit the shared file to
accommodate one machine.

If either path does not exist, say so rather than guessing at the conventions;
this machine may not be set up yet (`~/dev/weft-fabric/bootstrap.sh`).
